---
const overviewCandidates = [
  { name: 'Component substrate', status: 'Web Components · standardized' },
  { name: 'Navigation mechanics', status: 'HTML Living Standard' },
  { name: 'Transition mechanics', status: 'Level 2 · editor’s draft' },
  { name: 'Reactive graph', status: 'TC39 Signals · Stage 1 proposal' },
  { name: 'DOM / control primitives', status: 'DOM Parts · exploratory; Open UI · research' },
];
---

<section class="framework-overview not-prose" aria-labelledby="framework-platform-overview-title">
  <header class="overview-intro">
    <p class="meta accent">Overview · ~2 min</p>
    <h2 id="framework-platform-overview-title">What moves into the browser—and what stays above it?</h2>
    <p class="overview-lead">
      The question is not whether frameworks disappear. It is whether mechanisms repeated across frameworks can become shared platform primitives, while frameworks and applications retain the choices that make those primitives useful together.
    </p>
  </header>

  <figure class="boundary-visual" aria-labelledby="framework-platform-boundary-title">
    <div class="overview-heading">
      <h3 id="framework-platform-boundary-title">The layer moves; the work remains</h3>
      <p class="meta">Working boundary · not a forecast</p>
    </div>

    <div class="overview-grid">
      <section class="platform-side">
        <p class="meta accent">Below · reusable mechanisms</p>
        <h4>Some shared primitives can sit beneath many architectures.</h4>
        <p>Component identity, navigation and transition mechanics; proposed reactive and DOM mechanisms.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="framework-side">
        <p class="meta">Above · situated decisions</p>
        <h4>An application still needs someone to coordinate them.</h4>
        <p>Scheduling, rendering, routing and data, server integration, compilation, and team conventions.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <figcaption>
      The boundary is a way to ask who owns a responsibility. It does not say that every candidate will become a standard or that frameworks go away.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="overview-section" aria-labelledby="overview-candidates-title">
    <h3 id="overview-candidates-title">Candidates are not commitments</h3>
    <p>
      These efforts have different scopes and maturity. The labels describe the cited work as reviewed on September 17, 2026—not universal browser support.
    </p>

    <ul class="candidate-list">
      {overviewCandidates.map((item) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{item.name}</strong>
          <span>{item.status}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="overview-section" aria-labelledby="overview-examples-title">
    <h3 id="overview-examples-title">Two useful separations</h3>
    <div class="example-grid">
      <div class="example signals-example">
        <p class="meta accent">Signals · proposed</p>
        <p>
          A common dependency graph and auto-tracking model might be reusable. Effect scheduling, ownership, batching, and rendering would still be framework choices.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="example components-example">
        <p class="meta">Web Components · standardized substrate</p>
        <p>
          Portable component identity and encapsulation already exist. They do not decide application state, routing, data, or server/client execution.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="overview-section open-question" aria-labelledby="overview-open-question-title">
    <h3 id="overview-open-question-title">The open question</h3>
    <p>
      There is no single standards roadmap or inevitable path from experiment to native feature. If more low-level work becomes shared, how much useful architecture—and which deliberate policy choices—remain above it? That is the paper’s working investigation, not a prediction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="overview-transition">
    <p>
      Continue with the responsibility boundary, the Signals and Web Components cases, and the evidence behind the other platform efforts.
    </p>
    <button class="overview-continue" type="button" data-reading-depth-open="full">
      Full Research · ~8 min <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </button>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
